# Current STL SHA256: e234eac47d090551358b808b81d81ceb68261a86291685cff154af62e0ac0340
# Manifest SHA256: e10d326d5d1b0d23171358e0bb66f9c02e3444de730ceb6b22e9580e889cf804
# Exact cached post-live --all findings; answers explain only the anchors of their class.

[sliver] This 0.393 mm high exposed cap is at the combined R6/root/strut junction, not a full-span 0.393 mm web. Exact STEP vertical sections 0.25/1.00/3.00 mm inward from the cached cap span Z 13.050–19.322,13.051–19.672,13.051–20.604, giving 6.27–7.55 mm joined stock in those sections. The nominal brace remains 7 by 4 mm. This is a terminating joint band, not a strength measurement.
alternatives: A10–12 mm local elbow pad of 4–6 mm thickness or a larger blended joint grows the section at this cap, but adds stock in the reviewed sample/removal envelope and requires a new clearance review. A larger solid root fills more of the open comparison structure. Cutting the exposed taper away removes its small cap without enlarging the joint.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/h-fluted-helix-and-keyed-fin-docks.step
click: x=-36.770 y=36.770 z=13.247

[sliver] This 1.00 mm exposed band is the top of the 7 by 4 mm brace above the 3.00 mm R6 landing. A STEP vertical section 0.25 mm inward spans Z 21.054–27.827; the band is not an isolated 1.00 mm spanning web. The R6 exterior source surface remains separate from this reference storage brace. No load/life acceptance is inferred.
alternatives: A4.00 mm landing covers more of the brace end but enlarges the joint within the reviewed top-key seating envelope. A10–12 mm elbow pad or broader blended joint adds stock and needs the sample/removal clearance rechecked. A separate rigid stand replaces this brace with more footprint and another member.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/h-fluted-helix-and-keyed-fin-docks.step
click: x=62.000 y=25.000 z=27.304

[sliver] This 0.800 mm exposed top band belongs to the 7 by 4 mm brace at the GR upper cup, above its 2.70 mm roof. STEP vertical sections 0.25/1.00/3.00 mm inward run from Z 101.05 to 104.534/104.493/104.374. Those sections show joined stock behind the terminating band; they do not qualify strength or handling life.
alternatives: A3.50 mm cup roof or 10–12 mm local elbow pad covers more of the brace end while adding stock in the reviewed upper grip-removal envelope. A larger blended joint likewise needs the world -X grip path rechecked. A separate upper stand adds a member and changes the open sculpture footprint.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/h-fluted-helix-and-keyed-fin-docks.step
click: x=-79.737 y=1.538 z=104.150

[ceiling] These are the GR storage cup's accessible internal roof at manufacturing Z 101.05 and exterior underside at Z 97.50. Its world -X opening and bottom aperture leave the source grip column removable. H is prepared separately with Tree (auto) supports; these faces are reachable from the open side/below with H unassembled. They are storage ceilings, not a support-off source-R6 surface. Physical support removal and stability are unmeasured.
alternatives: An open top cradle removes the internal cup roof but changes its locating/capture arrangement. A tapered supported landing reduces print-down area while changing the grip footer contact. Larger continuous stock below the cup obstructs the independently removable grip column and needs a new motion review.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/h-fluted-helix-and-keyed-fin-docks.step
click: x=-81.250 y=1.500 z=101.050
click: x=-79.750 y=1.500 z=97.500

[ceiling] This 38 by 24 mm underside at manufacturing Z 23.05 is the 3.00 mm fin-dock platform, open underneath before assembly. H prints separately with automatic tree supports and all outside sides/bottom remain accessible for removal. The three A specimens retain their own manufacturing orientations and removable static peg seats; this platform is storage stock.
alternatives: A thicker platform adds stock upward/downward and needs the stored R45 roof/fin clearance reviewed. Sloped buttresses reduce the ceiling but occupy the open access under the fins. Separate individual pedestal blocks print with fewer elevated roofs while adding parts and changing the connected form.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/h-fluted-helix-and-keyed-fin-docks.step
click: x=0.000 y=31.000 z=23.050

[ceiling] These are the R6 upper landing and its inset storage key, at manufacturing Z 23.804 and 21.054. Their 14 by 10.8 mm /8 by 6 mm faces are part of H's removable vertical seating, distinct from the source R6 exterior transition. H uses automatic tree supports and both undersides are reachable from open sides/below before assembly. Nominal contact/path checks do not qualify physical removal force.
alternatives: A tapered key or open side hook changes its contact and vertical seating geometry. A solid column down to the bed eliminates this local ceiling but occupies the source R6/show-round access. A thicker broad landing adds stock within the reviewed top-dock envelope and needs new seating/neighbor checks.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/h-fluted-helix-and-keyed-fin-docks.step
click: x=62.000 y=25.000 z=23.804
click: x=62.000 y=25.000 z=21.054

[ceiling] This 40 by 7 mm underside at manufacturing Z 2.75 is the fluted apron above its two root keys, mating the basin at world Z 6.20. The apron has 7.00 mm wall stock with 1.20 mm show grooves and 5.80 mm intact backing; H is separately supported and the underside is open before assembly. It is not a 0.75 mm roof or a support-off contact sample.
alternatives: Continuing the apron solidly to the bed removes the ceiling but changes its mating root-key/basin seating. A broad sloped underside can reduce support while changing that bearing envelope. A separate bedded apron adds a joint and one more piece to the continuous carrier.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/h-fluted-helix-and-keyed-fin-docks.step
click: x=-36.800 y=46.500 z=2.750

[thin] These picks are local cut-ring-pod, apron and brace unions in H's lower root, including the first ribbon end. Their smallest regions are real tapered/corner stock at the unions, not an accepted thin-wall material gauge. The root joins the 7 by 4 mm braces,3.00 mm pod side stock and 7.00 mm apron; representative cap sections 0.25–3 mm inward retain 6.27–7.55 mm vertical joined stock. Automatic supports and the open H-only assembly provide access, but loss of small boundary material/cleanup and handling life remain unmeasured.
alternatives: A larger 10–12 mm elbow pad 4–6 mm thick or a broad blended root adds stock at these junctions while consuming open source-sample/removal space that needs renewed review. A full solid root block removes local tapers but replaces the open view/access with more material. Trimming the small projecting ends removes their tips without enlarging the underlying joint.
file: hardware/printed-parts/calibration/soda-studies/parts/h-fluted-helix-and-keyed-fin-docks.step
